The earlier solution was incorrect. The key error was the power-of-2 condition: in \((n-1)n(n+1)\), if \(n\) is even then only \(n\) is even, so we need \(n\equiv0\pmod4\); if \(n\) is odd, the two neighboring even numbers automatically supply at least \(2^2\). Thus the mod-4 condition is \(n\not\equiv2\pmod4\). The factor \(3\) is automatic, and the factor \(5\) requires \(n\equiv0,1,4\pmod5\).

Modulo \(20\), there are \(3\) valid residues mod \(4\) and \(3\) valid residues mod \(5\), giving \(9\) valid residues per block of \(20\). From \(1\) to \(100\) there are \(5\) full blocks, so the count is \(9\cdot5=45\).

